---
import EditorialLayout from "../layouts/EditorialLayout.astro";
import InquirySection from "./InquirySection.astro";
import { SEO } from "../lib/seo";
import "../styles/pages/streetwear-manufacturer.css";
---

<EditorialLayout
  seo={SEO["/streetwear-manufacturer/"]}
  navCurrent={["/production/", "/streetwear-manufacturer/"]}
  showMobileInquiry
>
<!-- ============ 1 HERO ============ -->
<section class="pd-hero" data-od-id="streetwear-hero">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-hero-grid">
      <div class="pd-hero-copy">
        <p class="label">B2B / OEM–ODM / Private label</p>
        <h1>Streetwear Manufacturing for Private Label Brands.</h1>
        <p class="lead pd-hero-lead">Build coordinated streetwear through silhouette, material, washes or decoration, private labels, sampling, and pack-out rules.</p>
        <div class="sw-hero-actions">
          <a class="button" href="/contact-us/">Send a streetwear brief</a>
          <a class="link" href="#manufacturing-scope">Review manufacturing scope</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="pd-hero-media">
        <img src="/uploads/streetwear-manufacturer/hero-streetwear-private-label.webp" width="1536" height="1024" alt="Private label streetwear range produced as a finished-garment reference" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>Supplied product reference · materials and specifications require confirmation</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ SCOPE STRIP ============ -->
<section class="st-strip" aria-label="Manufacturing workflow" data-od-id="page-section-index">
  <div class="shell">
    <span>Product definition</span>
    <span>Sample approval</span>
    <span>Private label</span>
    <span>Quality points</span>
    <span>Pack-out</span>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 01 PRODUCT OVERVIEW ============ -->
<section class="section band-alt" aria-labelledby="streetwear-overview-title" data-od-id="sw-overview">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">01 / Product overview</p>
        <h2 id="streetwear-overview-title">Custom Streetwear Manufacturing by Yauum</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">A strong streetwear program starts with a clear shared picture of what's being built. Fit, fabric, construction, branding, sample approval, packing, and delivery all need to be agreed on before pricing or production begins, so both sides are working from the same page instead of guessing at what the other means.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-cards sw-cards--3">
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">01 / PRODUCT</span><h3>Product Category and Style Direction</h3><p>Let us know the streetwear category and general style direction you're working toward, whether that's oversized tees, cargo pants, or layered outerwear. This gives us a starting reference point to build from instead of a blank page.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">02 / VOLUME</span><h3>Estimated Quantity and Color Breakdown</h3><p>Share a rough sense of the quantity you're planning and how it splits across colors. Even an estimate helps us think through cutting, dyeing, and how the production schedule should be laid out.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">03 / FIT</span><h3>Target Market and Size Range</h3><p>Tell us who the piece is for and what size range it needs to cover, along with any measurements you already have in mind. The fit conversation starts from here.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">04 / BRAND</span><h3>Branding and Packing Requirements</h3><p>Artwork, labels, trims, tags, and how the finished pieces should be packed all shape the final look and feel of the product, so it helps to bring these details in early rather than adding them after sampling has already started.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">05 / DELIVERY</span><h3>Delivery Destination and Timeline</h3><p>Where the order is heading, when you'd like it to arrive, and any documents needed for customs or retail compliance all affect how the schedule gets planned, so it's worth flagging these upfront rather than partway through.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 02 BUYER FIT AND SIZING ============ -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="streetwear-fit-title" data-od-id="sw-fit">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">02 / Buyer fit and sizing</p>
        <h2 id="streetwear-fit-title">Streetwear Fit and Sizing</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">Fit tends to shape everything that comes after it, from the pattern to the grading to how a fabric actually behaves once it's on the body. That's why it makes sense to settle fit before locking in construction or material decisions.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-cards sw-cards--3">
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">01 / SHAPE</span><h3>Silhouette and Proportions</h3><p>We work through the intended shape together, whether that's slim, relaxed, oversized, or boxy, along with how the proportions and drape should sit once worn.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">02 / GRADING</span><h3>Size Range and Grading</h3><p>Once the base shape is agreed on, we define the size range and the grading logic used to scale that pattern consistently across every size in the run.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">03 / MEASUREMENTS</span><h3>Measurement Points and Tolerances</h3><p>Key measurement points like chest, length, sleeve, and waist get identified together, along with a tolerance range you're comfortable with for each one, so later quality checks have something concrete to measure against.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 03 MANUFACTURING SCOPE ============ -->
<section class="section band-alt" id="manufacturing-scope" aria-labelledby="streetwear-scope-title" data-od-id="sw-scope">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">03 / Manufacturing scope</p>
        <h2 id="streetwear-scope-title">Streetwear Fabric, Construction, and Decoration</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">None of these decisions really stand on their own. Fabric, construction, and decoration all influence each other, which is why it helps to plan them together rather than one at a time before sampling begins.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-grid sw-grid--2">
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal><span class="sw-num">01 / FABRIC</span><h3>Fabric Composition and Weight</h3><p>We go through composition, weight, color, handfeel, stretch, drape, surface texture, and care instructions together, since these details end up affecting almost every other decision downstream.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal><span class="sw-num">02 / BUILD</span><h3>Seams, Stitching, and Reinforcement</h3><p>Seam types, stitching style, reinforcement points, lining, and finishing standards get documented clearly, so the factory floor has exact instructions to build from rather than assumptions.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal><span class="sw-num">03 / TRIMS</span><h3>Pockets, Closures, and Trims</h3><p>Pocket types, closures, zippers, drawcords, and hardware trims are confirmed together, since even small trim choices can shift both cost and the overall feel of the finished piece.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-block" data-reveal><span class="sw-num">04 / ARTWORK</span><h3>Artwork, Print, and Embroidery</h3><p>We help you choose between artwork applications like screen print, embroidery, puff, or appliqué, and coordinate the placement so it works well alongside labels and tags instead of competing with them visually.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 04 FACTORY EVIDENCE ============ -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="streetwear-proof-title" data-od-id="sw-proof">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">04 / Factory evidence</p>
        <h2 id="streetwear-proof-title">Streetwear Product and Factory Evidence</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">The supplied reference gives this page its starting anchor. The additional positions below are where real production photos and documents will go in as they become available, so buyers can see actual output rather than stock imagery.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="st-gallery" data-reveal>
      <figure class="st-gallery-main">
        <img src="/uploads/products/streetwear-manufacturer.webp" alt="Full streetwear product reference" width="844" height="1448" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>01 / FULL GARMENT REFERENCE</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="st-slots">
        <div class="sw-ph" aria-label="Fabric face and inside detail of a confirmed streetwear garment" data-evidence-status="needs_company_confirmation"><div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">02 / REQUIRED</span></div><div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Fabric Face and Inside Detail</strong><small>Recommended 1600 × 1200</small></div></div>
        <div class="sw-ph" aria-label="Close-ups of seams, pockets, and closures on a confirmed streetwear garment" data-evidence-status="needs_company_confirmation"><div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">03 / REQUIRED</span></div><div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Workmanship Close-Ups (seams / pockets / closures)</strong><small>Recommended 1600 × 1200</small></div></div>
        <div class="sw-ph" aria-label="Artwork, print, and label detail on a confirmed streetwear garment" data-evidence-status="needs_company_confirmation"><div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">04 / REQUIRED</span></div><div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Artwork, Print, and Label Detail</strong><small>Recommended 1600 × 1200</small></div></div>
        <div class="sw-ph" aria-label="Packing and carton reference photos for a confirmed streetwear order" data-evidence-status="needs_company_confirmation"><div class="sw-ph-media"><span class="sw-ph-tag">05 / REQUIRED</span></div><div class="sw-ph-copy"><strong>Packing and Carton Photos</strong><small>Recommended 1600 × 1200</small></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-spec">
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">01</span><div><h3>Full Garment Reference</h3><p>A complete front and back view of the referenced streetwear style, giving a full sense of the silhouette before getting into construction detail.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">02</span><div><h3>Fabric Face and Inside Detail</h3><p>A close-up of the fabric face alongside the inside construction, so material and build quality can be judged side by side rather than guessed at from a distance.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">03</span><div><h3>Workmanship Close-Ups</h3><p>Detail shots of seams, pockets, closures, and overall workmanship, meant to show the level of finishing a buyer can actually expect on the finished product.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">04</span><div><h3>Packing and Carton Photos</h3><p>Folding, bagging, and carton reference photos taken from a confirmed production run, giving a realistic sense of how the finished order will actually look once packed.</p></div></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 05 SAMPLE APPROVAL ============ -->
<section class="section band-dark" aria-labelledby="streetwear-sample-title" data-od-id="sw-sample">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label label--onDark">05 / Sample approval</p>
        <h2 id="streetwear-sample-title">Streetwear Sample Approval</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">A sample takes everything discussed on paper and turns it into a real garment, something the buyer can measure, wear, photograph, comment on, revise, and eventually sign off on before bulk production begins.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-spec">
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">01</span><div><h3>Fit and Measurement Review</h3><p>We look at shape, length, width, and proportions together, checking them against the confirmed spec so any adjustments are caught before cutting starts on the full order.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">02</span><div><h3>Fabric and Handfeel Review</h3><p>The sampled fabric gets assessed for handfeel, drape, surface, color, and how it's likely to wear over time, since these qualities are hard to judge from a swatch alone.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">03</span><div><h3>Workmanship and Trim Review</h3><p>Seams, stitching, closures, reinforcement, hardware, and finishing all get checked closely, since these details are usually where quality differences show up first.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">04</span><div><h3>Brand Application Review</h3><p>Artwork scale, placement, print or embroidery quality, labels, and tags are reviewed together to make sure the branding reads the way it was intended once it's actually on the garment.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">05</span><div><h3>Packing Instruction Review</h3><p>Folding, bags, inserts, stickers, and packing count are confirmed against the original plan, so there are no surprises once the order reaches the buyer's warehouse.</p></div></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 06 ORDER PROCESS ============ -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="streetwear-path-title" data-od-id="sw-path">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">06 / Order process</p>
        <h2 id="streetwear-path-title">Streetwear Manufacturing Order Process</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">A realistic timeline only comes together once the product, materials, customization, quantity, revisions, packing, and destination are all understood. Trying to quote before that tends to create more back-and-forth later, not less.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-spec st-steps">
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">01 / REVIEW</span><div><h3>Product Brief and Requirement Review</h3><p>We start by clarifying category, quantity, target market, references, size range, fabric, branding, packing, and destination, so everyone is working from the same brief from day one.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">02 / DEFINE</span><div><h3>Specification and Tech Pack</h3><p>Fit, measurements, materials, workmanship, trims, artwork, labels, and pack-out all get pulled together into one shared specification that both sides can refer back to throughout the project.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">03 / SAMPLE</span><div><h3>Sample Development and Revision</h3><p>The sample gets made, comments get documented clearly, and anything that isn't approved yet gets revised before moving forward.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">04 / BULK</span><div><h3>Bulk Production</h3><p>Once the sample is approved, that reference along with the order notes guides cutting, sewing, decoration, and finishing across the full run.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">05 / HANDOFF</span><div><h3>Final Check and Packing</h3><p>Finished garments, labels, unit packing, and carton details all get reviewed together as part of preparing the shipment.</p></div></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 07 QUALITY CONTROL ============ -->
<section class="section band-dark" aria-labelledby="streetwear-quality-title" data-od-id="sw-quality">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label label--onDark">07 / Quality control</p>
        <h2 id="streetwear-quality-title">Streetwear Quality Control</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">Acceptance points should be agreed on for the specific order and checked directly against the approved product reference, rather than judged against a generic quality standard that doesn't reflect this particular piece.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-spec">
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">01</span><div><h3>Material and Color Inspection</h3><p>Fabric and trims get compared against the approved direction to catch any drift in color or material before it becomes a bigger issue.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">02</span><div><h3>Measurement and Construction Check</h3><p>Dimensions, seams, stitching, pockets, closures, and overall workmanship are reviewed carefully against the confirmed spec.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">03</span><div><h3>Decoration and Label Verification</h3><p>Artwork placement, how the application actually looks, label type, and attachment method all get checked together.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">04</span><div><h3>Finished Garment Comparison</h3><p>The finished garment gets compared directly against the approved sample for fit, appearance, finish, and detail.</p></div></article>
      <article class="sw-spec-row" data-reveal><span class="sw-spec-num">05</span><div><h3>Packing and Carton Verification</h3><p>Folding, bags, tags, packing count, carton marks, and reference photos are all verified before the order ships.</p></div></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 08 PRIVATE LABEL ============ -->
<section class="section band-alt" aria-labelledby="streetwear-packing-title" data-od-id="sw-packing">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">08 / Private label</p>
        <h2 id="streetwear-packing-title">Private Label Branding for Streetwear</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">Brand details tend to matter more than they first appear. They shape how the product looks on a shelf, how it gets counted, and how smoothly it moves through receiving, so it's worth settling these early rather than treating them as an afterthought.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-cards sw-cards--3">
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">01 / GARMENT</span><h3>Woven and Neck Labels</h3><p>Woven, neck, size, and care labels get produced from the content and placement you supply, so the finished garment carries your brand correctly from the very first unit.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">02 / MERCHANDISE</span><h3>Hangtags and Barcodes</h3><p>Hangtags, stickers, barcodes, and any warehouse references you need get incorporated according to your specifications.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">03 / ARTWORK</span><h3>Artwork Placement and Application</h3><p>Artwork position, application method, and how it scales across different sizes all get confirmed together, so the branding stays consistent across the whole size run.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 09 PACKING AND PACK-OUT ============ -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="streetwear-packout-title" data-od-id="sw-packout">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label">09 / Packing and pack-out</p>
        <h2 id="streetwear-packout-title">Streetwear Packing and Pack-Out</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">How a product gets packed ends up shaping how easily it's counted, received, and distributed once it leaves the factory, so these instructions are worth nailing down clearly rather than leaving to default assumptions.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sw-cards sw-cards--3">
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">01 / UNIT</span><h3>Folding and Polybag Requirements</h3><p>Folding method, polybags, inserts, and stickers per unit are all specified, so the finished pieces arrive presented the way you intended.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">02 / CARTON</span><h3>Carton Marks and Size Ratios</h3><p>Carton quantity, size mix, carton marks, and handling instructions get confirmed to keep receiving and distribution running smoothly on your end.</p></article>
      <article class="sw-card" data-reveal><span class="sw-card-num">03 / PROOF</span><h3>Packing Reference Photos</h3><p>Packing photos and carton information from a confirmed production run give a realistic sense of what to expect once the shipment actually arrives.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 10 RELATED CATEGORIES ============ -->
<section class="section band-dark" aria-labelledby="streetwear-related-title" data-od-id="sw-related">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="pd-head">
      <div class="pd-title" data-reveal>
        <p class="label label--onDark">10 / Related categories</p>
        <h2 id="streetwear-related-title">Build a Coordinated Apparel Program.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pd-note" data-reveal>
        <p class="lead">Review adjacent product groups with the same structured development approach.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="st-related">
      <a href="/hoodie-manufacturer/">
        <img src="/uploads/products/hoodie-manufacturer.webp" alt="Hoodie Manufacturer product reference" width="844" height="1448" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <h3>Hoodie</h3>
        <span>View manufacturing page →</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/t-shirt-manufacturer/">
        <img src="/uploads/products/t-shirt-manufacturer.webp" alt="T-Shirt Manufacturer product reference" width="844" height="1448" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <h3>T-Shirt</h3>
        <span>View manufacturing page →</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/jackets-manufacturer/">
        <img src="/uploads/products/jackets-manufacturer.webp" alt="Jackets Manufacturer product reference" width="844" height="1448" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <h3>Jackets</h3>
        <span>View manufacturing page →</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 11 START AN INQUIRY ============ -->
<InquirySection
  product="Streetwear"
  id="sw-inquiry"
  odId="sw-inquiry"
  band="band-alt"
  kicker="11 / Start an inquiry"
  title="Send the Product Details That Shape a Useful Review."
  titleId="streetwear-inquiry-title"
  ctaLabel="Send project details"
/>
</EditorialLayout>
